(* ============================================================================
   PART 10: HTML SERIALIZATION CONVENTIONS (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   These conventions define the bytes the corpus pins. A corpus pair that
   disagrees with them is corrected under the preamble's NORMATIVITY rule.

   1. ATTRIBUTE ORDER. An element's attributes are written in FIVE RANKS, in
      this order:

      R1 STRUCTURAL. What the element derives from its OWN marker,
         destination or resolution: `<ol>`'s `type` and `start` (PART 9 §11
         N2) and then its `data-delim` (§12), a link's `href`, an image's
         `src` and `alt`, a link's or image's `title`, a footnote reference's
         `href` and `role`, a task item's `data-task-state` (§11) and, under
         roundtrip export, the carriers of §13, which follow the element's
         other structural attributes.
      R2 A MANDATORY BASE CLASS: math's `math inline|display`, an admonition's,
         a task list's `task-list` (§11), a figure group's `carve-figure-group`
         and a panel's `carve-figure-panel` (PART 9 §4c), and a core div's
         directive-kind class, the type word of its opener (`::: foo` gives
         `foo`). `line-block` is
         not a directive-kind class; it is R4's marker-derived class. ON AN
         ADMONITION AND A CORE DIV the directive's classes hold this rank whether
         or not the author wrote a class, and the authored classes follow them
         in that one slot: `{k=v .c}` over `::: note` gives
         `class="admonition note c" k="v"`. On a task list, where the author
         wrote a class, the base class is prepended inside that class slot
         instead, and so is math's: `` $`a`{k=v} `` gives
         `class="math inline" k="v"`, while `` $`a`{k=v .c} `` gives
         `k="v" class="math inline c"`. Math's classes stay on its own span.
      R3 AUTHORED, in the author's source order (the recorded `order`; PART
         4 RENDER ORDER). All classes accumulate, space-joined, into one
         `class` slot at its first-appearance position. An Attrs built
         programmatically with no recorded order falls back to `class`, then
         `id`, then key=value order. A code fence's header title (CARVE-P2-005)
         is authored: it takes its source position after the block-attribute
         line above the fence, so `{.x}` over ```` ```php "f" ```` gives
         `<pre class="x" title="f">`.
      An authored `href` or `src` on a link or image does not replace the
      URL derived at R1. The structural URL is emitted once; the colliding
      authored URL attribute is omitted. This applies to every R1 attribute
      the element derives: the structural value is emitted once and an authored
      attribute of that name is omitted. An attribute the element does not
      derive stays authored: `[a](/u){title=t}` has no structural title and
      `{start=9}` over `1. x` has no structural `start`, so both keep theirs.
      R4 GENERATED. What the engine mints on top of the authored set: the
         auto-slug id an unwrapped heading carries (CARVE-P9-019) and, where
         the author wrote no class, a marker-derived class such as
         `line-block` or a diagram fence's class, and an extension wrapper's
         `toc`, `glossary`, `index`, `tabs` or `code-group` class. Where the
         author did write a class, the marker-derived class is appended after
         the authored classes inside that class slot: `{.verse}` over
         `::: |` gives `class="verse line-block"` (carve#2328, carve#2338).
      R5 A RENDER ANNOTATION. The one in the core is `data-source-line` (the
         `sourceLine` render option), which records where a block was written.

      HTML ATTRIBUTE NAMES COMPARE ASCII CASE-INSENSITIVELY IN EVERY MERGE. A name
      written twice in any spelling is emitted once, at the first occurrence's
      position and spelling, with the last value, so `{K=1 k=2}` gives `K="2"`.
      The one exception is a code fence's header title: it is sugar for a
      title the block-attribute line above did not set, so a `title` on that
      line wins (CARVE-P2-005). `{title="outer"}` over ```` ```js "inner" ````
      gives `<pre title="outer">`.
      The `class` and `id` slots fold the same way: `{CLASS=z}` is the class
      slot, and `{CLASS=z}` over `::: |` gives `CLASS="z line-block"`. The R1
      omission above compares names the same way.

      An authored attribute replaces a generated attribute of the same name,
      compared ASCII case-insensitively. The generated one is omitted. These
      examples are quoted, so their headings do not wrap (CARVE-P9-019):

        > {k=v .attr}  -> <ol type="a" k="v" class="attr">
        > a. alpha
        > {a=b .c}     -> <h1 a="b" class="c" id="Auto">
        > # Auto
        > {#x a=b}     -> <h1 id="x" a="b">
        > # Written
        > ## Nested    -> <h2 id="Nested" data-source-line="1">

      THE RANK IS PROVENANCE, NOT THE ATTRIBUTE'S NAME. An id the author wrote
      keeps its authored position; an id the engine minted follows the
      authored run. A footnote reference is the exception: its id, minted or
      authored, leads before `href` (corpus 475). An element with no effective attributes emits none.

      A top-level heading's id moves to <section> and its other attributes do
      not (CARVE-P9-019); with the `sections` option off, or for a heading
      inside a container, the id stays on the <h*> with the rest:

        {#x a=b}       -> <section id="x"><h1 a="b">
        # Written

      A MENTION OR TAG IS THE STATED EXCEPTION: its structural class leads and
      the link form's `href` is second (PART 3, ATTRIBUTES ON A MENTION OR
      TAG).

      DIRECTIVE CLASSES FOLLOW ELEMENT OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-011].
      With no authored class slot, a directive-kind class leads on a core
      `<div>` and trails on an element rendered by an extension. If the
      extension abstains, core div rendering applies. The index extension
      abstains when there are no terms, so an empty index renders
      `<div class="index" id="ix" k="v">`, while a populated index
      renders `<ul id="ix" k="v" class="index">`.

      TEXT-BLOCK ALIGNMENT IS A SEMANTIC ATTRIBUTE. On `<p>`, `<div>` and
      `<h1>` through `<h6>`, an authored `align` key, matched
      ASCII-case-insensitively, whose trimmed,
      ASCII-case-folded value is `left`, `right` or `center` serializes as
      `style="text-align: VALUE;"`, not as the deprecated `align` attribute.
      The declaration takes the authored `align` slot. If the author also
      supplied `style`, emit one `style` attribute at its authored slot and
      append the declaration there: the authored value trimmed, a `;` added
      if it does not already end in one, one space, then the declaration. An
      empty trimmed value adds nothing, neither `;` nor space:
      `{style="" align=right}` gives `style="text-align: right;"`. The appended declaration
      wins if that style already named `text-align`. The value is the one
      left after CARVE-P9-054 hardening, so a style that hardening blanks
      still takes the declaration: `{style="url(x)" align=right}` over `p`
      gives `<p style="text-align: right;">`, as a table cell does.

      A TABLE CELL'S ALIGNMENT MERGES THE SAME WAY. A cell's `text-align` and
      `vertical-align` declarations (PART 9 §5) are appended to an authored
      `style` at its authored slot, as above, so `|>{style="color:red"} a |`
      gives `<td style="color:red; text-align: right;">a</td>`.

      The element and value sets are closed. On `<table>`, `align` means block
      placement rather than alignment of cell text, so it remains an ordinary
      attribute. Every element outside the text-block set, and every value
      outside the three named values, likewise keeps the authored attribute.
      Rewriting a table's placement as `text-align` would change the document,
      not modernize an equivalent spelling.

   2. ATTRIBUTE QUOTING + ESCAPING. Attribute values are double-quoted.
      In an attribute value escape `&`->&amp;, `<`->&lt;, `>`->&gt;,
      `"`->&quot;, `'`->&apos;. In text/content escape `&`,`<`,`>` (NOT
      quotes). ONE EXCEPTION: a NO-BREAK SPACE (U+00A0) in text content
      serializes as `&nbsp;`, the one entity beyond the three, so it
      renders visibly distinct from an ordinary space with no external
      CSS -- matching the escaped-space spelling `\ ` (CARVE-P12-004) and a line
      block's preserved indentation (PART 9 §23), both of which already
      serialize the same way. No other entities are produced. This
      describes only the QUOTING of values that survive filtering:
      dangerous attribute names, values, and URL schemes are
      dropped/blanked FIRST per PART 9 §25.

   3. VOID ELEMENTS are written WITHOUT a trailing slash: `<hr>`,
      `<img …>`, `<br>`. A hard break serializes as `<br>` + newline.

   4. BLOCK WHITESPACE. Block elements are newline-separated, one per
      line. Nested block structures (list, blockquote, table, admonition,
      div, figure, section, endnotes) indent their children by TWO spaces
      per nesting level. Inline content stays flat on its block's line.
      A blockquote whose only visible child is an uncaptioned paragraph
      writes that paragraph directly between its tags on one line, with no
      child indentation or inserted newline: `<blockquote><p>q</p></blockquote>`.
      Invisible children do not prevent this compact form. A block image,
      captioned paragraph, or second visible block takes the multiline form.

      A BLOCK IMAGE (CARVE-P9R-007) is a bare `<img …>` on its own line, with
      no `<p>` around it. A captioned host other than a table is a `<figure>`
      holding the host element and then its `<figcaption>`, and the host's
      block attributes, its id included, go on the `<figure>` (corpus
      47-numbered-cross-references-8). A table keeps its attributes on
      `<table>` and holds its caption as `<caption>` (§7).

      The HTML API returns a fragment with no final LF, except a matching raw
      block's payload that ends the document: it drops only its last newline,
      and an all-blank payload drops none (PART 2, raw blocks). A caller
      writing an output file may add its own line ending after the fragment.

      AN EMPTY CONTAINER BODY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-001]. A container whose body renders
      nothing keeps a BLANK LINE where the body would be:

        <blockquote>

        </blockquote>

      This includes a bare `:::` div, a div with a word class, an admonition,
      a blockquote, a figure group, a line block and a local hard-break block.
      The container kind does not change the body slot. A body that renders to
      nothing is an empty body -- a container holding only a comment takes the
      same shape as one holding nothing. A rendered caption or div label is
      visible container content and therefore fills the body slot. PART 9 §23
      turns a comment-only line in a line block into an empty verse paragraph;
      that paragraph is visible body content (`<p></p>`), not an empty body.

      A MATCHING RAW BLOCK KEEPS ITS PLACEMENT SLOT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-012].
      A zero-line raw payload and one blank payload line are distinct under
      PART 2 raw_block. A matching raw block whose payload has zero lines emits one
      line: an empty one at the top level, and one holding only the body's
      indentation in a nested body.
      That placement counts as visible content for [CARVE-P10-001] and
      [CARVE-P9-077]; it keeps a child slot beside a paragraph in a list item.
      A comment or a raw block for another target contributes no slot. These
      placement rules do not loosen a list; PART 9 §17 L1 governs tightness.

      A PLACED ELEMENT'S OWN TAGS CARRY THE PER-IMPLEMENTATION INDENTATION -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-010]. A `directive` (PART 12 §35) places an element around content the
      extension generates -- `<nav class="toc">`, `<dl class="glossary">`,
      `<ul class="index">`, a degraded `<div class="{kind}">`. The two halves are pinned
      differently.

      THE OPENING AND CLOSING TAG may carry the nesting indentation above or may sit at
      column 0. Both conform and neither is pinned, so a consumer comparing two
      implementations does not compare those two lines' leading whitespace. ONE COLUMN
      SERVES BOTH TAGS: an element whose opener and closer sit at different columns
      conforms under neither choice.

      THE GENERATED CONTENT between them is the cross-implementation contract,
      BYTE-IDENTICAL including its own leading whitespace. Where it is anchored is the
      extension's own statement rather than the marker's column: `::: toc` writes the
      `<ul>`, its items and the closing `</ul>` at column 0 wherever the marker was
      written, while a `glossary` row and an `index` item sit one level inside their own
      element and move with the column it took. A consumer compares the generated lines
      modulo that one column and nothing else.

        <section id="Heading">
          <h1>Heading</h1>
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <ul>
        <li><a href="#Heading">Heading</a></li>
        </ul>
        </nav>
        </section>

      `toc`, `glossary` and `index` are governed by this one wording. A kind's own
      section in `docs/extension-contract.md` fixes where its generated lines sit and
      may not redraw this boundary.

   5. PARAGRAPH WRAPPING follows the tight/loose rule (PART 9 §17): a tight list
      item's text has no <p>; a loose item's paragraphs are wrapped.

   6. CODE. A fenced code block is `<pre><code[ class="language-X"]>` +
      escaped literal `code_block.content` + `</code></pre>` (PART 12 §3).
      In static HTML, a diagram whose renderer is absent falls back to an
      ordinary code block. It emits the unchanged payload, including an
      authored final LF before `</code>`, under the same rule.
      No payload newline is added. An inline
      code span is `<code>` + escaped content + `</code>`.

      AN ESCAPED RAW BLOCK IS WRITTEN AS CODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-013].
      A raw block that a safe policy escapes rather than omits serializes
      exactly as a fenced code block whose language is the raw block's
      recorded `format`, verbatim. The escape is not a render loss; a policy
      that omits the block still reports `raw-format-dropped` ([CARVE-P2-024]).

        > ```=html      ->  <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p&gt;raw&lt;/p&gt;
        > <p>raw</p>        </code></pre>
        > ```

   7. TABLES. Alignment renders as `style="text-align: VALUE;"` (one
      space after the colon, trailing semicolon). Vertical alignment adds
      `vertical-align: VALUE;` to the same `style`, after `text-align` and
      one space from it when both are present: `style="text-align: right;
      vertical-align: top;"` (PART 12 §19 and §20). A cell with no effective
      alignment on either axis emits NO style attribute. `rowspan`/`colspan` are emitted
      only when their count is > 1.
      A visible resolved rowspan that crosses a row-group boundary cannot occupy its
      authored slots if those groups become separate HTML sections. In that
      case the whole table uses one `<tbody>` containing every row, in source
      order, instead of separate `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` sections.
      Cell kind, `scope`, alignment and the resolved span count are computed
      from the original partition; only the HTML section wrappers change.
      This applies to derived and explicit partitions and to ListTable body
      groups. Tables with no crossing rowspan keep their sections.
      A ROW IS A ROW, IN EVERY SECTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-002]. `<thead>`,
      `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` are each written on their own lines with ONE ROW
      PER LINE, indented one level inside the section:

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">a</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

   8. STABLE STRINGS. Mentions and tags render as inert `<span>`s (no link)
      when no URL template is configured; a configured template links to them.
      The visible sigil and name form one `<strong>` child inside the span
      (PART 3, CARVE-P3-027): `<span class="mention"><strong>@ann</strong></span>`
      and `<span class="tag"><strong>#tag</strong></span>`. The footnote
      backlink glyph is `↩` (CARVE-P9-025). Math wraps content in `\(…\)` /
      `\[…\]` inside `<span class="math inline|display">` (CARVE-P9-036).

   9. SEMANTIC SPAN ATTRIBUTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-003]. On an ordinary `[content]{attrs}`
      span, three attribute names are reserved HTML rendering sugar: `abbr`,
      `time` and `kbd`. Each present name is consumed as an attribute and wraps
      the rendered inline content in its same-named element.

        `Press [Tab]{kbd} to indent.`        ->  `<kbd>Tab</kbd>`
        `[HTML]{abbr="HyperText Markup Language"}`
                                             ->  `<abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr>`
        `[now]{time="2026-01-01"}`           ->  `<time datetime="2026-01-01">now</time>`

      A non-empty `abbr` or `time` value becomes `title` or `datetime`; a value
      on `kbd` only selects the wrapper. A DERIVED ATTRIBUTE YIELDS TO AN
      AUTHORED ONE OF THE SAME NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-004]. `title` and `datetime` are
      ordinary attribute names an author may also write, so
      `[x]{abbr="gen" title="authored"}` is `<abbr title="authored">`, not two
      `title` attributes on one element. The author wrote that one in the
      output's own vocabulary; the derived one is a convenience for the case
      where they did not. Several names nest in this fixed
      INNER-TO-OUTER order, independent of the order the author typed them:
      `abbr`, `time`, `kbd`.

      LEFTOVER ATTRIBUTES RIDE THE OUTERMOST SEMANTIC ELEMENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-005].
      Where a semantic name is present, the span does not survive as a wrapper:
      every remaining id, class and key/value attribute lands on the outermost
      semantic element and passes through §1-2 plus PART 9 §25 hardening
      there.

        `[Tab]{#k .key kbd}`    ->  `<kbd id="k" class="key">Tab</kbd>`
        `[x]{#i time="12:00" kbd}`
                                 ->  `<kbd id="i"><time datetime="12:00">x</time></kbd>`
        `[x]{kbd onclick="e"}`   ->  `<kbd>x</kbd>`
        `[x]{}`                  ->  `<span>x</span>`
        `[x]{onclick="e"}`       ->  `<span>x</span>`

      A span with no semantic name renders its `<span>` whether or not
      anything survives PART 9 §25 hardening, which removes attributes and
      never the element (corpus `108-security-hardening-8` and `-10`).

      THE REGISTRY HOLDS NO ELEMENT CARVE ALREADY SPELLS INLINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-006].
      `code` and `mark` are not reserved: a code span writes `<code>` and the
      highlight syntax writes `<mark>`. `abbr` is reserved although a
      document-level `*[HTML]: …` definition also emits `<abbr>`: the
      definition expands every occurrence of its term, and the attribute
      marks one.

      THE CORE SEMANTIC NAMES ARE CLOSED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-007]. Core
      recognizes exactly `abbr`, `time` and `kbd`. `samp`, `var`, `cite` and
      `dfn` belong to the SemanticSpan extension described in §10.

      An explicit `abbr` attribute is authoritative over automatic abbreviation
      expansion inside that span. A resolved `abbreviation` child contributes
      only its visible abbreviation text there; renderers MUST NOT emit a
      nested `<abbr>`. Thus a document-level `*[HTML]: Long` definition does not
      override `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}`.

      This rule changes only HTML rendering. The AST remains an ordinary `span`
      carrying the authored attributes, plain and ANSI targets render its
      inline content, and the canonical writer emits the span with its
      attributes, an empty-string one spelled bare per CARVE-P11-012.

      Accessible ruby is NOT reserved here: PART 12 §32 gives it structured
      base/annotation children rather than a single inline container.

   10. THE OTHER SEMANTIC NAMES ARE AN EXTENSION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-008]. `samp`, `var`,
      `cite` and `dfn` are not core: a conformant core renders `[x]{samp}` as an ordinary
      boolean attribute (`<span samp="">x</span>`) and `:samp[x]` through the
      generic `<span class="ext-samp">` fallback.

      They are the `SemanticSpan` extension, Tier-2 and off by default, whose
      contract is `docs/extension-contract.md` §11: when it is enabled the four names
      behave exactly as §9's three do, under the same nesting order, the same
      value mapping for `dfn`, the same attribute riding, and the same
      hardening. The extension also accepts `:name[…]` for all seven names;
      writers use the compact spelling.

      A core implementation MUST NOT turn an arbitrary extension name into an
      HTML tag: outside `SemanticSpan`, every `:name[…]` keeps the generic
      fallback. `cite` denotes a work title or source and is unrelated to a
      bibliographic `[@key]` citation, which is the Citations extension's.

   11. A TASK LIST AND ITS NAMED TASK STATES NAME THEMSELVES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-009].
      PART 2 admits nine task marker spellings and the rendered checkbox has two
      positions. So that the authored state reaches the output, the item
      carries `data-task-state` holding the authored character:

        - [-] dropped   ->  <li data-task-state="-"><input type="checkbox"
                            disabled aria-label="dropped"> dropped</li>

      A DONE ITEM NAMES ITS STATE TOO. `[x]` and `[X]` are one state, so both
      carry `data-task-state="x"`, always the lowercase `x`, and one selector
      (`li[data-task-state="x"]`) reaches a done item's text without `:has()`.
      AN OPEN SPACE ITEM STAYS BARE: `[ ]` carries no attribute. `[_]` is the
      same open state but retains `data-task-state="_"` to preserve spelling.
      The AST does not change: `checked` records the done state and `taskState` stays absent
      for `[ ]`, `[x]` and `[X]` (PART 12), so the renderer writes `x` from
      `checked`.

      THE LIST CARRIES `task-list`. A task list's `<ul>` carries the
      mandatory base class `task-list` (§1): it leads when the author wrote no
      class and is prepended inside the authored class slot when one exists.
      PART 9 §11 N1 makes plain-vs-task a same-list axis, so a list is all
      task items or none and the class reaches every item. For an imported or
      supplied AST, derive this class only on a nonempty unordered list whose
      direct items all have a checkbox. Mixed and ordered lists gain no derived
      task-list class. An authored task-list class remains authored when that
      shape is absent. A plain list nested
      inside a task item is not a task list and stays bare:

        - [ ] open       ->  <ul class="task-list">
        - [x] done             <li><input type="checkbox" disabled
                               aria-label="open"> open</li>
                               <li data-task-state="x"><input type="checkbox"
                               checked disabled aria-label="done"> done</li>
                             </ul>

      ON THE `<li>`, NOT ON THE `<input>`. The state is a property of the
      ITEM -- PART 2 says so of the checkbox itself -- and a stylesheet
      reaching the item's own text is what the attribute is for
      (`li[data-task-state="-"]`). Putting it on the box would put the
      selector one level below the content it wants to reach.

      IT IS A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE and leads the authored ones (§1), like
      `<ol>`'s `type` and a link's `href`: the item derives it from its own
      marker rather than from anything the author attached. When a structural
      state is derived, suppress an authored attribute with the same
      ASCII-case-insensitive name so the item has one state attribute. Without
      a derived state, authored attributes retain their existing behavior.

      THE VALUE IS THE RECORDED CHARACTER, escaped as §2 escapes any value --
      `>` is written `&gt;`, and a value outside PART 2's enumeration is not
      one this clause writes.

      AN IMPORTER CONSUMES IT, and consuming is not the same as dropping a
      DERIVED attribute (CARVE-P9-065). The checkbox `<input>` is already
      consumed into the item's state rather than kept as content; this
      attribute is the other half of that state and is read the same way. It
      is what lets a document survive a render-and-import cycle: without it
      `[-]` comes back as `[ ]`, silently, because both spellings render the
      same box and no diagnostic can see a difference that is not there.
      `data-task-state="x"` beside a checked box is the done state, consumed
      with the box rather than kept as an authored attribute, and the list's
      `task-list` class is consumed as a structural class: neither comes back
      in the imported source.

      THE ACCESSIBLE NAME DOES NOT MOVE. The box keeps the item's own text as
      its `aria-label` (PART 2). Naming the state there would invent copy the
      author did not write, and the states are an author's shorthand rather
      than a vocabulary this spec can translate.

   12. AN ORDERED LIST'S DELIMITER NAMES ITSELF ON THE LIST -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-014]. PART 2 admits `.` and `)` after an ordered marker, and
      `type` and `start` (§1) do not say which the author wrote. The list
      carries `data-delim` holding the authored character:

        1) first   ->  <ol data-delim=")">
                         <li>first</li>
                       </ol>

      ONLY WHERE THE AST RECORDS A DELIMITER. PART 12 §3 records `list.delim`
      only where it is not the default: a `.` is absent there and carries no
      attribute here, and such a list's bytes are unchanged by this clause.

      ON THE `<ol>`, NOT ON THE `<li>`. The delimiter is a property of the
      LIST, fixed once by its first item's marker (PART 9 §11 N2), so a nested list
      derives its own from its own marker and does not inherit the attribute.

      IT IS A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE and leads the authored ones (§1), beside
      `<ol>`'s own `type` and `start`, which it trails: the list derives it
      from its own marker rather than from anything the author attached.

      THE VALUE IS THE RECORDED CHARACTER, escaped as §2 escapes any value,
      and a value outside PART 2's enumeration is not one this clause writes.

      AN IMPORTER CONSUMES IT, as §11 says of `data-task-state` and for the
      same reason: without it a rendered `1)` imports back as `1.`. A
      value outside PART 2's enumeration is an author attribute, not a
      delimiter.


   13. ROUNDTRIP EXPORT WRITES NAMED CARRIERS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-015].
      §1 to §12 describe the bytes every render writes. A document also holds
      distinctions a target's serialization has no spelling for, and once a
      render drops one no import can recover it. ROUNDTRIP EXPORT is one
      opt-in that adds the carriers this clause names and changes nothing
      else:

        PHP renderer          setRoundTripMode(true)
        JavaScript options    roundtrip: true
        Rust options          roundtrip: true / with_roundtrip(true)
        CLI                   --roundtrip

      THE CARRIER MODEL IS THIS ONE TABLE. Each row is a distinction, the
      carrier the HTML target writes for it and the carrier the Markdown
      target writes for it. `none` means the target writes no carrier for the
      row; it says nothing about what the target's own syntax keeps. A
      Markdown export carries its own column and is not a claim to preserve
      what the HTML column lists.

        DISTINCTION            HTML CARRIER                     MARKDOWN CARRIER
        `list.bulletChar`      `data-marker` on `<ul>`: the     none
                               authored bullet, written only
                               where it is not `-`
        `thematic_break.       `data-char` on `<hr>`: the       none
        marker`                authored character, written
                               only where it is not `-`
        an authored heading    `data-carve-explicit-id`,        the attribute-line
        `id` under `attrs`     presence, on the `<section>`     marker
                               where CARVE-P9-019 wraps one     (CARVE-P11-067)
                               and on the `<h1>`-`<h6>` where
                               it does not
        that id's slot in      `data-carve-id-slot` on          inside the
        `attrs.order`          `<section>`, beside              attribute-line
                               `data-carve-explicit-id`: the    marker
                               index of `#id` among the
                               heading's attributes
        a generated heading    `data-carve-generated-id`,       none
        id                     presence, on the host
                               `data-carve-explicit-id` would
                               take
        a link's or image's    `data-carve-ref` on `<a>` or     a link's native
        `ref` and `rawRef`     `<img>`: the reference label     reference form and
                               it resolved by                   its definition
                                                                line (CARVE-P11-028)
        an `autolink` node     `data-carve-autolink` on `<a>`,  none
                               presence
        `footnote_ref.label`   `data-carve-footnote-label` on   none
                               the reference `<a>` (its `<sup>`
                               inside link text) and the
                               endnote `<li>`: the authored
                               label, which [CARVE-P12-047]
                               names and spells, not `id`
        an inline footnote     `data-carve-inline-footnote`     none
                               on the endnote `<li>`, presence
        a `raw_inline` and     `data-carve-raw` on `<span>`:    none
        its format             the format; the span's TEXT
                               holds the content
        an `escaped_text`      `data-carve-escaped` on          none
        node                   `<span>`, presence; the span
                               holds the one escaped
                               character
        the abbreviation       `data-carve-abbreviations` on    none
        definitions            `<template>`, presence; its
                               TEXT holds one
                               `*[ABBR]: expansion` per line
        `table.                `data-carve-col-widths` on       none
        separatorWidths`       `<table>`: the separator-row
                               dash widths, comma-joined, one
                               per separator cell
                               ([CARVE-P11-066]). The field's
                               PRESENCE is the distinction: a
                               table with no separator row
                               writes no attribute
        `code_block.label`     `data-carve-label` on `<pre>`:   the fence marker
                               the text between the fence       (CARVE-P11-067),
                               label's brackets; a block with   which also holds
                               no label writes no attribute     the title
        `list.tight`, where    `data-carve-tight` on `<ul>` or  none
        an item's paragraph    `<ol>`, presence
        keeps its `<p>`
        (CARVE-P9-081)
        a resolved             `data-carve-xref` on `<a>`,      the
        `heading_ref`          presence; the `href` fragment    cross-reference
                               is its `target`                  marker
                                                                (CARVE-P11-068)
        a link's, an image's   none                             the attribute
        or a span's                                             marker, or the
        attributes, and a                                       span's opener
        span                                                    and closer
                                                                (CARVE-P11-068)
        a `caption_number`     `data-carve-caption-number` on   inside the
                               the `<span>` that wraps the      caption marker
                               number, presence                 (CARVE-P11-067)
        a `div` whose          `data-carve-div` on the          the container
        `<div>` carries no     `<div>`, presence                markers
        other attribute                                         (CARVE-P11-063)
        a `line_block`         `data-carve-line-block` on the   the container
                               line block's `<div>`, presence   markers
                                                                (CARVE-P11-063)
        a `substitution`       `data-carve-substitution` on a   none
                               `<span>` that wraps the node's
                               `<del>` and `<ins>`, presence
        an element-less       none                             the opener and
        container: its kind,                                    closer markers
        title, attributes                                       (CARVE-P11-063)
        and nesting
        a block's attribute    none                             the attribute-line
        line that reaches no                                    marker (CARVE-P11-067)
        element of the
        Markdown output
        a definition list,     none                             the term, `:` and
        outermost only                                          closing markers
                                                                (CARVE-P11-067)
        a caption line         none                             the caption marker
                                                                (CARVE-P11-063, CARVE-P11-067)

      THE SHARED RULES hold for both columns:

      S1 OFF BY DEFAULT on every surface. The default bytes are the ones §1
         to §12 and PART 11 pin, and enabling the option neither removes nor
         reorders anything those sections write.
      S4 A VALUE OUTSIDE ITS GRAMMAR IS NOT A CARRIER, AND IT IS REPORTED.
         Nothing is rebuilt from it, and only that carrier falls with it, except
         that an invalid Markdown opener marker falls together with its closer
         (CARVE-P10-017, CARVE-P11-063). A Markdown marker set
         whose opener and closer pairing breaks reads the whole file as plain
         Markdown (CARVE-P11-063).
      Trust, contradiction and import-mode handling: CARVE-P10-017,
      CARVE-P10-018, CARVE-P10-021 and CARVE-P11-063.

      WHAT IT GUARANTEES IS RECOVERY OF THE NAMED DISTINCTIONS, not
      byte-identical source. Every HTML carrier names a field or a node type
      PART 12 already carries, so the distinction is recoverable from the
      tree whether or not HTML carried it: the carrier exists because this
      serialization collapses the distinction, not because the tree lost it.
      A DISTINCTION THE AST DOES NOT RECORD GETS NO HTML CARRIER, and the way
      to add one is to add the PART 12 field FIRST. That is what makes the
      HTML column DERIVABLE: a reader checks it against the tree instead of
      trusting the list. THE MARKDOWN CONTAINER MARKERS FOLLOW THE SAME RULE:
      each holds the opener or closer the canonical writer spells, so no
      authored fence width or padding is carried (CARVE-P11-063).

      WHAT IT DOES NOT PROMISE IS A RAW BLOCK. CARVE-P10-016 names no carrier for one,
      so a raw block whose format this target cannot write is dropped under
      this option exactly as it is without it, and the render reports the
      dropped format as a diagnostic in both cases. ENABLING THE OPTION NEVER
      SUPPRESSES THAT DIAGNOSTIC: a preservation mode that loses a block
      silently is worse than a default one that says so. NO CARRIER IS
      DEFINED FOR ONE, AND THAT IS A STATED LIMIT on this target and on the
      Markdown target alike. A carrier would put arbitrary foreign content
      into an attribute or a comment, the target can never render those
      bytes, and roundtrip import is not a trust decision (CARVE-P10-017).

      A RAW INLINE REPORTS THE SAME DIAGNOSTIC AND IS CARRIED. A raw inline
      whose format this target cannot write reports `raw-format-dropped` with
      the option on or off. Under the option the `data-carve-raw` carrier is
      written beside that report, not in place of it.

      WHAT IT DOES NOT RECOVER IS A REFERENCE'S FORM. The collapsed `[r][]`
      and the full `[r][r]` both write `data-carve-ref="r"`, and no carrier
      holds which of the two the author wrote. A reference whose form is not
      recovered returns as `[r][]`.

   13a. THE HTML CARRIERS ARE THE NAMES IN THIS INVENTORY -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-016]. THE INVENTORY is the HTML column of §13's table. Under
      roundtrip export, and only under it, the CORE HTML target writes those
      names and no other preservation name.

      AN ORDERED LIST'S DELIMITER IS `data-delim`, NOT A SECOND CARRIER. §12
      writes the authored delimiter on every render, and an ordered list's
      marker IS that delimiter: its style is `type` and its first number is
      `start`, both structural and both already written. An `<ol>` carrying
      both names says one thing twice, so `data-marker` rides a `<ul>` only.

      THE ABBREVIATION CARRIER PRESERVES EVERY AUTHORED DECLARATION. Its
      template text holds the definitions in source order, including every
      superseded definition. A reader takes duplicate carrier lines in that
      order, so the last definition for a term is effective for rendering
      (PART 9R R3). Neither export nor import deduplicates the source lines.

      A CARRIER IS STRUCTURAL (§1 R1). It sits after the element's other
      structural attributes (`type`, `start`, `data-delim`, `href`, `src`,
      `alt`, `title`; §16 R1) and before the first attribute the author wrote, and
      the placement does not vary by carrier:
        > {.c #i}
        > ***            -> <hr data-char="*" class="c" id="i">

      A LABEL VALUE IS THE `label` PRODUCTION'S INTERIOR. `data-carve-label`
      holds what PART 2 writes between `[` and `]`, so a reader validates it
      against that production before rebuilding the fence label: a value that
      holds `]` or a line break is not a carrier, and CARVE-P10-017's rule
      for an invalid value applies. An empty value is a carrier: it is the
      empty label `[]`, which the AST keeps as `label: ""`, and a reader
      rebuilds `[]`.

        ```php [Example]     ->  <pre data-carve-label="Example"><code
        echo 1;                  class="language-php">echo 1;
        ```                      </code></pre>

      AN UNMARKED ID ON A HEADING IS AUTHORED. Where an element carries
      neither `data-carve-explicit-id` nor `data-carve-generated-id`, a reader
      keeps a heading's id as `{#id}`. A `<section>` wrapper's id that equals
      the heading's PART 2 slug is generated; any other wrapper id is authored.

      AN ID SLOT IS A RUN OF DECIMAL DIGITS. The slot indexes the heading's
      attributes in their authored order and counts from 0. A writer MUST omit
      the carrier at 0, where the id leads. A reader places the id at that
      slot. A slot larger than the heading's attribute count, like any other
      value, is not a carrier (CARVE-P10-017) and the id leads.

        {.c #mine}      ->  <section id="mine" data-carve-explicit-id
        # H                 data-carve-id-slot="1">

      A CROSS-REFERENCE'S TEXT IS GENERATED, SO THE IMPORT DISCARDS IT. The
      reader rebuilds `</#id>` from the `href` alone, which MUST be `#`
      followed by a value the `crossref_id` production (PART 3) admits; any
      other `href` is not this carrier (CARVE-P10-017). An unresolved
      cross-reference renders no `<a>` and takes no carrier.

        See </#fig-sun>.   ->  <p>See <a href="#fig-sun"
                               data-carve-xref>Figure 1</a>.</p>

      A CAPTION NUMBER IS WRAPPED ONLY UNDER ROUNDTRIP EXPORT. The default
      render writes the number as bare text; the carrier wraps exactly that
      number and nothing else, and the reader replaces the span with
      `caption_number` whatever digits it holds. A span with any other
      attribute or with element content is not this carrier.

        ^ Figure #: The sun  ->  <figcaption>Figure <span
                                 data-carve-caption-number>1</span>: The
                                 sun</figcaption>

      A BARE DIV IS MARKED, NOT INFERRED. A `<div>` with no attribute is what
      any foreign HTML holds, so a reader never rebuilds a div from such an
      element alone. It rebuilds a `div` from a `<div>` that carries `data-carve-div`,
      and a writer writes the carrier only where the `<div>` carries no other
      attribute.

        :::            ->  <div data-carve-div>
        inside             <p>inside</p>
        :::                </div>

      A LINE BLOCK IS READ FROM ITS CARRIER, NOT FROM ITS CLASS. The admonition
      `::: line-block` renders the same `<div class="line-block">` as a line
      block and carries no carrier, so a `<div class="line-block">` without
      `data-carve-line-block` is never read as a line block. A reader rebuilds a
      `line_block` from a `<div>` that carries the carrier.
      An authored `line-block` class on a line block is not recovered: the
      renderer folds it into the class the line block writes anyway.

        ::: |          ->  <div data-carve-line-block class="line-block">
        first              <p>first<br>
        second             second</p>
        :::                </div>

      A SUBSTITUTION IS ONE WRAPPED PAIR. The carrier's `<span>` holds exactly
      one `<del>` followed by one `<ins>`, and the reader rebuilds one
      `substitution` from them, `old` from the `<del>` and `new` from the
      `<ins>`. A span with any other attribute or any other content is not
      this carrier. A `<del>` followed by an `<ins>` outside the carrier stays
      a deletion and an insertion, as an authored `{-old-}{+new+}` writes them.

        a {~old~>new~} b  ->  <p>a <span data-carve-substitution><del>old</del><ins>new</ins></span> b</p>

      A CONSTRUCT'S VERBATIM SOURCE IS WHAT THE RULE STILL EXCLUDES, which is
      why `data-carve-src` is absent from the rows above. It is not awaiting a
      field: [CARVE-P11-013] rules the glued opener canonical and says the tree
      records no padding, fence length or fence character, so the one
      distinction such a carrier is observed to move -- the space in
      ``` ``` php ``` -- is a spelling this specification has already ruled
      against keeping. A field for it would reverse that clause rather than add
      to it.

      AN EXTENSION WRITES ITS OWN CARRIERS, under the reserved prefix, for the
      distinctions ITS nodes record. They are scoped to that extension and
      enumerated in that extension's own contract rather than here, which is
      what the CORE qualifier above leaves room for. An extension's contract
      MAY place a core carrier name on its own element, and defines its
      meaning there. AN EXTENSION CARRIER IS A
      CARRIER in every other respect: CARVE-P10-019 displaces an authored attribute of
      its name, CARVE-P10-020 reads its deprecated spelling, and CARVE-P10-021 consumes it in
      every import mode.

      A WIDTH IS A POSITIVE INTEGER, AND 1000 IS THE BOUND ON BOTH SIDES OF
      THE CARRIER. The widths arrive as a comma-joined list in an attribute an
      importer reads off UNTRUSTED HTML, and the writer spells one `-` per
      unit, so six characters of attribute buy a megabyte of separator row.
      That is an amplification the SOURCE side does not have -- an author who
      types a thousand dashes has already paid for them -- so the bound
      belongs on the carrier rather than on the field.

      A READER REFUSES THE WHOLE CARRIER -- writing no field at all, which
      means the `|=` form -- where any entry is empty, contains whitespace,
      or is not a run of decimal digits, or
      where any entry is below 1 or exceeds 1000. Not a partial read and not a
      clamp to the bound: both put a separator row on the page whose widths
      nobody wrote,
      and refusing leaves the table correct in its other spelling.

      THE READER COMPARES THE DECIMAL VALUE WITHOUT INTEGER OVERFLOW. Leading
      zeros do not change that value; a writer emits ordinary decimal integers.

      A WRITER WRITES NO CARRIER where the field is absent, empty, or holds an
      entry below 1 or above 1000, for the same reason read the other way: a carrier a conforming reader will
      refuse is a round-trip promise the writer cannot keep. The FIELD itself
      has no upper bound, because a parse records what the author typed and no
      amplification happens there.

      A VALUE ESCAPES AS §2 ESCAPES ANY ATTRIBUTE VALUE. §2's text-content
      exception for U+00A0 is not an attribute rule, so a carrier value
      holding a no-break space keeps that character.

      A CARRIER THAT HOLDS ITS PAYLOAD AS CONTENT ESCAPES IT AS §2 ESCAPES TEXT
      CONTENT, and a reader takes the element's TEXT rather than its serialized
      markup. `data-carve-raw`, `data-carve-escaped` and
      `data-carve-abbreviations` are the three, and for `data-carve-raw` this is
      not a formatting preference: written unescaped, a payload of `<b>` opens a
      tag the span never closes and the rest of the paragraph disappears into
      it. Reading the serialized markup back is the same mistake from the other
      side, because it escapes the payload a second time and a raw inline
      holding `&` returns holding `&amp;`.

      A PRESENCE CARRIER CARRIES NOTHING IN ITS VALUE.
      `data-carve-explicit-id`, `data-carve-generated-id`,
      `data-carve-autolink`, `data-carve-inline-footnote`,
      `data-carve-escaped`, `data-carve-tight`, `data-carve-xref`,
      `data-carve-caption-number`, `data-carve-div`,
      `data-carve-line-block` and `data-carve-substitution` say what they say
      by being there. A writer writes the empty value or `1`; a reader reads
      presence and does not compare the value.

      ONE ESCAPE PER ELEMENT. A `data-carve-escaped` span holds exactly ONE
      escaped character. A writer never collects a run of escapes onto one
      span and a reader never reassembles one, because a span holding `abc`
      would import as `\a\b\c`, which is not Carve. A span carrying more than
      one character is MALFORMED and the importer takes its content as text.
      Reporting it is permitted, not required: an importer that builds a tree
      without a diagnostic channel still has to read the span.

      THE RESERVED SET IS AN ENUMERATION, NOT A PREFIX PATTERN. These names
      are the serializer's protocol rather than an author's content, and each
      one is reserved because it is named: a row of this inventory, the
      rowless name below and its alias, or an extension carrier its own
      contract names. So `data-marker` and `data-char` keep their own spelling
      and are no less reserved for carrying no prefix, and a `data-carve-`
      name none of these names is NOT a carrier and NOT reserved: an engine
      does not invent one, and a reader treats it as an ordinary authored
      attribute. Adding a name to this inventory is a change to this clause.

      ONE NAME IS RESERVED WITHOUT A ROW. The core target writes no
      `data-carve-src`, and an importer consumes it in every mode and rebuilds
      nothing from its value. So under roundtrip export an authored one is
      dropped with CARVE-P10-019's `carrier-attribute-dropped` row, and default export
      passes it through untouched.

      THE SPEC'S OWN ATTRIBUTES ARE NOT CARRIERS. A task list's `task-list`
      class and its items' `data-task-state` (§11), and an ordered list's
      `data-delim` (§12), are written by every render, with or without this
      option, and each has its own clause. This option neither gates nor
      duplicates them.

   13b. ROUNDTRIP EXPORT DROPS AN AUTHORED RESERVED NAME AND REPORTS IT --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-019]. Under roundtrip export an authored attribute
      whose name is a reserved name from CARVE-P10-016, or the deprecated `data-djot-`
      spelling of one, which CARVE-P10-020 has every importer consume, IS DROPPED ON
      EVERY NODE, whether or not that node writes a carrier of that name. A node that
      writes a carrier of that name still writes it. Default export passes
      the authored attribute through.

        [a](/u){data-carve-ref=x}   ->  <a href="/u">a</a>

      THE DROP IS A RENDER-LOSS ROW NAMING THE ATTRIBUTE, so the document
      records the loss instead of taking it silently. The code is
      `carrier-attribute-dropped` and the row carries `attribute`, the dropped name;
      [CARVE-P2-024] states the rest of its shape.

      EMITTING BOTH IS NOT CONFORMING. Two attributes of one name is invalid
      HTML, and the precedence does not vary by carrier: the rule is the same
      for every name in the inventory. REFUSING THE EXPORT IS NOT CONFORMING
      EITHER -- one authored attribute cannot veto a mode the caller asked
      for.

   13c. AN IMPORTER READS THE DEPRECATED `data-djot-` SPELLING -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-020]. The prefixed names in CARVE-P10-016 were spelled `data-djot-`
      before this clause, and documents exported by released engines carry
      that spelling. So, for every prefixed name in the inventory:

        A WRITER EMITS `data-carve-` ONLY. The `data-djot-` spelling is never
        written, under this option or any other.

        AN IMPORTER ACCEPTS EITHER SPELLING, and `data-carve-` wins where one
        element carries both. `data-djot-` is DEPRECATED: it is read for
        compatibility and consumed exactly as CARVE-P10-021 consumes the canonical
        spelling, so a document that arrives carrying it leaves without it.

      THE ALIAS IS NOT A SECOND SET OF NAMES. A `data-djot-` name this
      inventory does not list under its `data-carve-` spelling is not a
      carrier and not reserved; it is an ordinary authored attribute.
      `data-marker` and `data-char` have no alias, having never carried a
      prefix.

      `data-djot-src` IS THE ONE ALIAS OF THE ROWLESS NAME. It is reserved and
      consumed like `data-carve-src` (CARVE-P10-016), in every import mode, and nothing
      is rebuilt from its value.

   13d. EVERY IMPORT MODE CONSUMES THE INVENTORY AND WRITES NO RESERVED NAME
      BACK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-021]. Consuming a carrier reconstructs
      syntax the AST can already express, so it is not a trust decision and
      is not gated on an import mode. `safe` import consumes every name in
      CARVE-P10-016, in either spelling, exactly as `roundtrip` import does.

      A RESERVED NAME NEVER LEAVES AS AN AUTHORED ATTRIBUTE. An importer that
      consumed a carrier does not then write its name into the reconstructed
      document's attributes: `<hr data-char="_">` imports as `___`, not as
      `---{data-char=_}`, and `<a href="/u" data-carve-ref="lbl">text</a>`
      imports as `[text][lbl]`, not as `[text](/u){data-carve-ref=lbl}`. A
      reserved name an importer has no use for is still consumed rather than
      passed through, with or without a diagnostic saying so.

      CONTRADICTORY CARRIERS RESTORE NEITHER MEANING. The contradictory
      pairs are a CLOSED LIST, each pair on one element:

        data-carve-footnote-label   with   data-carve-inline-footnote
        data-carve-autolink         with   data-carve-ref
        data-carve-explicit-id      with   data-carve-generated-id
        data-carve-escaped          with   data-carve-raw
        data-carve-xref             with   data-carve-ref
        data-carve-xref             with   data-carve-autolink
        data-carve-xref             with   data-carve-footnote-label

      For a pair on the list the reader consumes neither carrier's meaning: it
      imports the ordinary element, reports one `attribute-dropped` warning
      for the pair, and excludes both reserved names from authored attributes.
      `data-carve-autolink` beside `data-carve-ref` on one `<a>` imports as an
      inline link. A PAIR NOT ON THE LIST IS NOT A CONTRADICTION. This applies
      in every import mode and to the deprecated aliases in CARVE-P10-020. Value
      validation under CARVE-P10-017 comes first: an invalid value is not a carrier
      and contradicts nothing.

      A `data-carve-id-slot` WITHOUT `data-carve-explicit-id` on the same
      element is not a carrier, and CARVE-P10-017 applies.

      THE FOOTNOTE PAIR IS THE FIRST INSTANCE. An endnote carrying both a
      valid `data-carve-footnote-label` and `data-carve-inline-footnote` asks
      for a labeled note and an inline note, which has no authored label, so
      it imports as the ordinary numbered reference and endnote body. A valid
      label alone and an inline flag alone retain their reconstruction, and an
      invalid label does not suppress a valid inline-footnote carrier. Shared
      mode and alias controls are in
      `resources/footnote-carrier-recovery-fixtures.json`.

      ONE CARRIER IS THE EXCEPTION. A `data-carve-raw` naming format `html`
      returns its payload as raw HTML only under CARVE-P10-017's mode gate.
      Otherwise the importer drops the carrier and takes the span's text.
      Other formats are consumed in every import mode.

      WHAT THE MODE DECIDES IS NOTHING ELSE. Every other name in CARVE-P10-016 is
      consumed whatever the mode, and reconstruction of raw HTML outside a
      carrier is CARVE-P10-017's question rather than this clause's.

      AN IMPORT PUBLISHES `attrs.order` ON A HEADING where its
      `data-carve-explicit-id` or `data-carve-id-slot` carrier supplies it.
      Elsewhere an import publishes no `order`.

   13e. EXPORT DOES NOT SELECT AN IMPORT MODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-017].
      Roundtrip export adds information to OUTPUT. HTML import's `roundtrip`
      mode decides reconstruction and raw-HTML preservation of INPUT, and
      finding one of these carriers in a document is not a reason to trust
      that document. Enabling export never enables a trusted import, never
      widens an importer's mode, and never relaxes a destination check.

      A CARRIER VALUE IS UNTRUSTED INPUT. Before reconstructing syntax, a
      reader validates the value against the grammar of the construct it
      rebuilds. Any normalization the writer applies is applied before the
      reader makes a trust decision about that value.

      A VALUE OUTSIDE ITS GRAMMAR OR CARRIER ENUMERATION IS NOT A CARRIER.
      The reader drops the invalid value with a warning and keeps the
      reserved name out of the reconstructed document's authored attributes
      (CARVE-P10-021). It does not rebuild syntax from an invalid value.

      RAW HTML REMAINS MODE-GATED. A `data-carve-raw` value naming format
      `html` reconstructs raw HTML only in `roundtrip` import mode. A carrier
      must not restore markup the selected mode filtered out. Other raw-inline
      formats, inert in HTML output, may reconstruct in every import mode.

   13f. ONE OPTION SWITCHES EVERY TARGET'S CARRIERS -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-018]. Roundtrip export is the only switch for the carriers
      of either target. An engine exposes no target-specific alias for it:
      PART 11's carrier mode IS this option on the Markdown target, so one
      setting cannot enable a target's carriers while another disables them.
      A narrower control, if one is ever wanted, is a separate explicit
      setting with a clause of its own.

   ============================================================================ *)
